# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets. This repository is PUBLIC — everything here is a one-mistake-away
# guard, so the patterns are deliberately wider than the exact filenames.
#
# The reason for the wildcards: scripts/install-native.ps1 and
# scripts/setup-gdrive-auth.ps1 keep a safety copy of the file they are about
# to rewrite, as "<name>.bak-<timestamp>", INSIDE the repo. A rule matching the
# literal name only (".env") does not match ".env.bak-20260731-120000", so
# those copies were untracked-but-not-ignored: a routine `git add -A` would
# have staged them, and with them DB_PASSWORD, ODOO_ADMIN_PASSWD,
# BACKUP_PASSPHRASE, GDRIVE_CLIENT_SECRET and CLOUDFLARE_TUNNEL_TOKEN — to a
# public repo. Nothing ever leaked (the whole history is clean); this closes
# the trap before it fires.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
config/odoo.native.conf*
# Google Drive OAuth token blob (created by scripts/setup-gdrive-auth.ps1)
config/gdrive-token.json.dpapi*
# Catch-all for any other rescue copy a script leaves behind.
*.bak
*.bak-*

*.pyc
__pycache__/
*.log
*.swp
.idea/
# .vscode: keep settings + recommended extensions, ignore user-specific files
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/extensions.json
backups/
*.dump
*.gpg

#agent
.claude/
CLAUDE.md

# Native-install artifacts (created by scripts/install-native.ps1)
.odoo/
.venv/
.runtime/

# docs/ is ignored by owner decision (internal working notes stay local). The
# docs that WERE committed before this rule are still tracked and still ship in
# a clone — git keeps tracking a file once it is in the index. So the published
# doc set is frozen at what was committed: new local docs are not published,
# and edits to the already-tracked ones still are. Left as-is deliberately;
# publishing the local-only notes to a PUBLIC repo is the owner's call, not a
# side effect of a .gitignore tidy-up.
docs/
# Local duplicate of docs/ (1.4 MB, ~97 files). Untracked AND unignored is the
# dangerous combination: `git add -A` would publish a second copy of the whole
# documentation set to a public repo without anyone deciding to.
documents/
